M3.9 isolated restore permission diagnosis
Date: 2026-08-27
Result: restore proof incomplete; production archive and database unchanged

The archive structure, checksum and pg_restore catalogue passed. The first
isolated restore attempt then stopped because mktemp created the throwaway
restore directory as root:root mode 0700 while pg_restore deliberately ran as
the unprivileged postgres account. The cleanup trap removed the empty
throwaway database, extracted dump and temporary directory.

The retry is limited to assigning that random restore directory and extracted
dump to root:postgres with modes 0750 and 0640. The production database is not
a restore target. The randomly named database is force-dropped and the exact
temporary files are unlinked after the invariant queries complete.

That retry still stopped because the postgres account cannot traverse the
private backup ancestors, which is an intentional separation and must not be
widened for test tooling. The final restore proof therefore has root read and
validate the dump, emits owner/privilege-free SQL on standard output and pipes
it directly to psql running as postgres with ON_ERROR_STOP. No dump copy or
backup-directory ACL is exposed to postgres.
The first streaming invocation omitted pg_restore's mandatory `--file=-`
selector and therefore exited before emitting SQL; the cleanup again removed
the empty throwaway database. The verified retry uses that explicit selector.
